 while the default literal search keeps its current [/#D7D7AF]\n[dim]         [#D7D7AF][/dim]substring semantics and compiles no regex at all.[/#D7D7AF]\n[dim] Counts: [bold #D7AFFF][/dim]3[dim][/bold #D7AFFF] phases[dim][/dim] · [bold #D7AFFF][/dim]3[dim][/bold #D7AFFF] waves[/dim]\n[dim]   Path: [#87AFFF][/dim]plans:202608/[bold #87AFFF][/#87AFFF]bead_search_regex.md[/bold #87AFFF]\n[dim] Prompt: [#87AFFF][/dim]prompts/202608/bead_search_regex.md[/#87AFFF]\n[dim]   Bead: [#87AFFF][/dim]sase-i1[/#87AFFF]\n[dim]   Page: [#87AFFF][/dim]https://github.com/sase-org/sase--beads/blob/main/pages/sase-i1/[bold #87AFFF][/#87AFFF]README.md[/bold #87AFFF]\n[dim]  1 [bold #AF87FF][/dim]◆ [bold #D7AFFF][/bold #AF87FF]Rust core regex matcher and fast-path flag[/bold #D7AFFF]                    [bold black on #FFD75F] medium [/bold black on #FFD75F]\n    [#AF87FF]core[dim] · [dim][/dim]no dependencies[/#AF87FF][/dim]\n    [#D7D7AF]core: add a shared bead query matcher to sase-core, thread an opt-in [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]`regex` argument through `search_issues`/`search_issues_in_issues` and [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]the `bead_search` PyO3 binding, teach the Rust bead CLI to parse [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]`-e`/`--regex` and to highlight and snippet regex matches, and cover all[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]of it with Rust tests.[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF][/#D7D7AF]\n[dim]  2 [bold #AF87FF][/dim]◆ [bold #D7AFFF][/bold #AF87FF]Adopt the released core in the sase dependency floor[/bold #D7AFFF]          [bold black on #87D7FF] small  [/bold black on #87D7FF]\n    [#AF87FF]floor[dim] · [dim][/dim]after core[/#AF87FF][/dim]\n    [#D7D7AF]floor: after sase-core publishes the release that carries the regex [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]binding, raise the `sase-core-rs` minimum in pyproject.toml, refresh [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]uv.lock, and confirm the published-core minimum smoke tooling accepts [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]the new floor.[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF][/#D7D7AF]\n[dim]  3 [bold #AF87FF][/dim